activities.content:
In Module 1, whenever we created a `Student` object, we had to manually set every field line-by-line:

```java
Student s = new Student();
s.name = "Rahul";
s.age = 20;
s.course = "Java";
```

What if you forgot to set `name`? It stayed `null`. What if you forgot `age`? It stayed `0`. An object with missing data is dangerous.

### The Constructor Solution
A constructor allows an object to initialize itself the moment it is born!

```java
class Student {
    String name;
    int age;

    // 1. Exact same name as class
    // 2. NO return type (not even void!)
    Student() {
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 18;
        System.out.println("Student object initialized!");
    }
}
```

### Two Golden Rules of Java Constructors:
1. **The Name Rule:** The constructor name must match the class name character-for-character (`Student()`).
2. **The Return Type Rule:** Constructors NEVER have a return type. If you write `void Student()`, Java treats it as an ordinary method, NOT a constructor!

### Step-by-Step Execution Sequence:
```text
Student s = new Student();
        │
        ├─► 1. 'new' requests RAM space for a new Student object
        ├─► 2. Fields default to null and 0
        ├─► 3. Java automatically calls Student() constructor
        ├─► 4. name = "Unknown", age = 18 are assigned
        └─► 5. Address of the initialized object is returned to variable 's'
```

activities.codeSnippet:
class Car {
    String brand;
    int speed;

    // Constructor runs automatically when 'new Car()' is called
    Car() {
        brand = "Generic Motors";
        speed = 0;
        System.out.println("🚗 Setup Wizard: Car engine initialized at 0 km/h");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before object creation...");

        // The constructor executes right here during 'new Car()'
        Car myCar = new Car();

        System.out.println("Brand: " + myCar.brand + " | Speed: " + myCar.speed);
    }
}

